  "content": {
    "id": "p22-q24-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "24",
    "prompt": "Show that the roots of $(5-x)(2-x)=p^2$ are always real and distinct.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\Delta=9+4p^2>0$ for every real $p$, so there are two distinct real roots.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(5-x)(2-x)&=p^2\\\\x^2-7x+10-p^2&=0\\\\\\Delta&=(-7)^2-4(1)(10-p^2)\\\\\\Delta&=9+4p^2\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $p^2\\ge0$ for real $p$, the discriminant is positive and there are two distinct real roots.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 25
  },
